"1706.1","Malik narrated to me from Yahya ibn Sa'id from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb that a man from Banu Mudlij, named Qatadah, struck his son with a sword, hitting his leg. The wound was cauterized and he died. Surakah ibn Ju'shum came to 'Umar ibn al-Khattab and informed him. 'Umar said to him: ""Count out 120 camels at the water of Qudayd until I come to you."" When 'Umar ibn al-Khattab arrived, he took from those camels 30 hiqqah (three-year-old she-camels), 30 jadh'ah (four-year-old she-camels), and 40 khulafah (pregnant she-camels). Then he said, ""Where is the brother of the slain?"" He said, ""Here I am."" He said, ""Take them, for the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'There is nothing for the killer.'"""
"1706.2","From Abu Umamah ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf that he said: 'Amir ibn Rabi'ah saw Sahl ibn Hunayf bathing and said: ""I have not seen the like of today, not even the skin of a maiden."" Sahl fell ill. He was brought to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and it was said: ""O Messenger of Allah, do you have anything for Sahl ibn Hunayf? By Allah, he cannot raise his head."" He said: ""Do you suspect anyone for him?"" They said: ""We suspect 'Amir ibn Rabi'ah."" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) called 'Amir, was angry with him, and said: ""Why does one of you kill his brother? Why did you not bless him? Perform wudu for him."" 'Amir washed his face, hands, elbows, knees, feet, and the inside of his lower garment in a vessel, then it was poured over him. Sahl went with the people, nothing ailing him. Muwatta Imam Malik, Hadith 1706."
