"1717.1","Yahya related to me from Malik that Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Sasaca said that he heard Abu'l-Hubab Said ibn Yasar say that he had heard Abu Hurayra say"
"1717.2","Yahya related to me from Malik that Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Sasaca said that he heard Abu'l-Hubab Said ibn Yasar say that he had heard Abu Hurayra say"
"1718.1","Yahya narrated to me from Malik from Yahya ibn Sa'id from Bushayr ibn Yasar that he informed him that 'Abdullah ibn Sahl al-Ansari and Muhayyisah ibn Mas'ud went to Khaybar. They separated for their needs. 'Abdullah ibn Sahl was killed. Muhayyisah came, and he, his brother Huwayyisah, and 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Sahl came to the Prophet (ﷺ). 'Abd al-Rahman began to speak because of his closeness to his brother. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Let the elder speak, let the elder speak."" So Huwayyisah and Muhayyisah spoke and mentioned the matter of 'Abdullah ibn Sahl. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Will you swear fifty oaths and thereby claim the blood of your companion or your killer?"" They said: ""O Messenger of Allah, we did not witness nor were we present."" He said: ""Then the Jews will exonerate themselves with fifty oaths."" They said: ""O Messenger of Allah, how can we accept the oaths of a disbelieving people?"" Yahya ibn Sa'id said: Bushayr ibn Yasar claimed that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) paid the blood money from his own wealth. Malik said: The agreed ruling with us, which I heard from those I trust, regarding qasama (oaths in homicide), and upon which the Imams agreed in past and present, is that the claimants begin the oaths in qasama. Qasama is only obligatory in one of two cases: either the slain says, ""My blood is with so-and-so,"" or the heirs of the blood bring a luth (circumstantial evidence) that is not conclusive against the accused. This obligates qasama for the claimants against the accused. Qasama is not obligatory with us except by one of these two ways. Malik said: This is the undisputed Sunnah with us, upon which people have always acted: the ones who begin qasama are the heirs of the blood, those who claim intentional or unintentional killing. Malik said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) began with the Harithis in the killing of their companion at Khaybar. Malik said: If the claimants swear, they are entitled to the blood of their companion and kill the one they swore against. No more than one is killed in qasama; two are not killed. Fifty men from the heirs of the blood swear fifty oaths. If their number is less or some refuse, the oaths are repeated upon them, unless one of the heirs of the slain who have the right to pardon refuses. If one of them refuses, there is no way to the blood. Yahya said: Malik said: The oaths are repeated upon those remaining only if the one who refused is not entitled to pardon. If one of the heirs of the blood who are entitled to pardon refuses, even if only one, the oaths are not repeated upon the remaining heirs. But in that case, the oaths are repeated upon the accused: fifty of them swear fifty oaths. If they are not fifty, the oaths are repeated upon those who swore. If only the accused is found, he swears fifty oaths and is exonerated. Yahya said: Malik said: The difference between qasama in blood and oaths in property rights is that when a man lends to another, he secures his right; but when a man intends to kill another, he does not kill him in a gathering, he seeks seclusion. If qasama were only where evidence is established, and conducted like property rights, blood would be lost and people would be emboldened when they know the judgment. But qasama was given to the heirs of the slain to begin, so that people may refrain from blood and the killer may fear being seized by the word of the slain. Yahya said: Malik said concerning a people who have an enemy suspected of the blood: the heirs of the slain repeat the oaths upon them, and they are a group with numbers. Each man swears fifty oaths for himself; the oaths are not divided proportionally to their number, nor are they exonerated until each swears fifty oaths for himself. Malik said: This is the best I have heard on this. Qasama belongs to the 'asaba (paternal relatives) of the slain; they are the heirs of the blood who swear and by whose qasama killing occurs."
